  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "While waiters compete to deliver room service to generous guests in room 202, the supposed inspector in room 313 rejects Chef Barinov's steak and demands coffee right to his face. Listen to the scene.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Новый заказ в наш номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Я сам сегодня это несу!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Какие там огромные чаевые!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "У меня таких чаевых ещё никогда не было.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хоть бы с друзьями поделился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Немедленно уберите это со стола.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Не люблю, когда мясо плохо прожарено.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас я всё быстро переделаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Уже не надо. Принесите кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он просто так сегодня шутит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Этому гостю очень повезло, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Такому обслуживанию позавидует сам президент.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Новый заказ в наш номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A new order for our room.' The grammar packs 'new' and 'number' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Я сам сегодня это несу!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am carrying this myself today!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'am bringing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Какие там огромные чаевые!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What huge tips they give in there!' In Russian, the idea of 'what' flows naturally into 'tips' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "У меня таких чаевых ещё никогда не было.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have never had tips like these before.' Senya anchors the line on 'at', while 'was' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хоть бы с друзьями поделился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You could at least share with your friends.' The grammar packs 'at least' and 'shared' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Немедленно уберите это со стола.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Immediately take this off the table.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'immediately' before landing on 'table'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Не люблю, когда мясо плохо прожарено.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not like it when the meat is poorly cooked.' The grammar packs 'not' and 'cooked through' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас я всё быстро переделаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Right now I will redo everything quickly.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'now', while 'I will redo' completes the thought. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Уже не надо. Принесите кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No need anymore. Bring some coffee.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'already' before landing on 'coffee'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он просто так сегодня шутит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is just joking like that today.' The meaning hinges on 'he' working together with 'jokes' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Этому гостю очень повезло, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This guest got really lucky, right?' Senya raises the pitch on 'yes' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Такому обслуживанию позавидует сам президент.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The president himself would envy service like this.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'such a' and finishing on 'president'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
